IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cbca-core`

-- Tests for cbca-core.l4. Expected values are written from the calendar and from the text's own
-- examples of time limits ("twenty days", "six months", "two years", "four weeks"), never copied from
-- the module's output. The Act does not say how a time limit is counted; the convention tested here is
-- fork F1 in NOTES.md: the start day is allowed, and the last day is the start plus the limit, included.

§ `Tests -- the shared vocabulary`

§§ `The YMD shim: year first, and the same date as Date day month year`

#ASSERT YearMonthDay 2026 9 30 EQUALS Date 30 9 2026
#ASSERT YearMonthDay 2024 2 29 EQUALS Date 29 2 2024

§§ `Time limits`

`twenty days` MEANS `Time limit` WITH
    `number of units` IS 20
    `unit of time` IS `calendar days`

`four weeks` MEANS `Time limit` WITH
    `number of units` IS 4
    `unit of time` IS `calendar weeks`

`six months` MEANS `Time limit` WITH
    `number of units` IS 6
    `unit of time` IS `calendar months`

`two years` MEANS `Time limit` WITH
    `number of units` IS 2
    `unit of time` IS `calendar years`

`sixty days` MEANS `Time limit` WITH
    `number of units` IS 60
    `unit of time` IS `calendar days`

`three months` MEANS `Time limit` WITH
    `number of units` IS 3
    `unit of time` IS `calendar months`

-- 1 March 2026 plus 20 days is 21 March 2026.
#ASSERT `the last day of` `twenty days` `counted from` (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 3 21
-- 25 February 2026 plus 20 days crosses a month end: 25 Feb + 3 = 28 Feb, + 17 more = 17 March.
#ASSERT `the last day of` `twenty days` `counted from` (YearMonthDay 2026 2 25) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 3 17
-- 1 January 2026 plus four weeks (28 days) is 29 January 2026.
#ASSERT `the last day of` `four weeks` `counted from` (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 1 29
-- 31 August 2025 plus six months: there is no 31 February, so the last day clamps to 28 February 2026.
#ASSERT `the last day of` `six months` `counted from` (YearMonthDay 2025 8 31) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 2 28
-- 31 January 2026 plus six months is 31 July 2026.
#ASSERT `the last day of` `six months` `counted from` (YearMonthDay 2026 1 31) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 7 31
-- 29 February 2024 plus two years clamps to 28 February 2026.
#ASSERT `the last day of` `two years` `counted from` (YearMonthDay 2024 2 29) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 2 28

-- Counting back: 60 days before 15 June 2026 is 16 April 2026 (30 days back is 16 May, 60 is 16 April).
#ASSERT `the earliest day allowed by` `sixty days` `counted back from` (YearMonthDay 2026 6 15) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 4 16
-- Three months before 31 May 2026 clamps to 28 February 2026.
#ASSERT `the earliest day allowed by` `three months` `counted back from` (YearMonthDay 2026 5 31) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 2 28
-- Two years before 29 February 2024 is 28 February 2022.
#ASSERT `the earliest day allowed by` `two years` `counted back from` (YearMonthDay 2024 2 29) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2022 2 28

-- Within the time limit "twenty days after 1 March 2026": the start day and the last day are in time,
-- the day after is not, and a day before the start is not.
#ASSERT `done within the time limit` `twenty days` (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1)
#ASSERT `done within the time limit` `twenty days` (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 21)
#ASSERT (NOT `done within the time limit` `twenty days` (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 22))
#ASSERT (NOT `done within the time limit` `twenty days` (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 2 28))
-- "six months after 31 August 2025": 28 February 2026 is in time, 1 March 2026 is not.
#ASSERT `done within the time limit` `six months` (YearMonthDay 2025 8 31) (YearMonthDay 2026 2 28)
#ASSERT (NOT `done within the time limit` `six months` (YearMonthDay 2025 8 31) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1))

§§ `Vocabulary: the constructors are distinct and compare`

#ASSERT (NOT (`an individual` EQUALS `a body corporate`))
#ASSERT (NOT (`a director` EQUALS `the Director`))
#ASSERT (NOT (`the president` EQUALS `the secretary`))
#ASSERT `Quebec` EQUALS `Quebec`
#ASSERT (NOT (`an ordinary resolution` EQUALS `a special resolution`))

§§ `Vocabulary: a vote tally and a block of shares are plain records`

`a tally of 3 for and 1 against` MEANS `Vote tally` WITH
    `votes cast in favour` IS 3
    `votes cast against` IS 1
    `signed by all the shareholders entitled to vote on the resolution` IS FALSE

`a block of 25 votes worth 500 dollars` MEANS `Block of shares` WITH
    `votes carried` IS 25
    `fair market value in dollars` IS 500

#ASSERT (`a tally of 3 for and 1 against`'s `votes cast in favour`) EQUALS 3
#ASSERT (`a block of 25 votes worth 500 dollars`'s `fair market value in dollars`) EQUALS 500
